Q1. What is Agile methodology, and why was it introduced?

Interview Ready Answer

Agile is a software development methodology based on iterative and
incremental development. It focuses on delivering software in small,
working increments while maintaining continuous collaboration with
stakeholders and adapting to changing requirements.

It was introduced to overcome the limitations of the Waterfall model,
where all requirements are finalized upfront and the product is delivered
only at the end. Agile enables continuous customer feedback, faster
delivery, and quick adaptation to changing business requirements.

Definition

Agile is a software development methodology that emphasizes iterative
development, incremental delivery, customer collaboration, and continuous
improvement.

Purpose

• Deliver working software frequently.
• Adapt to changing requirements.
• Improve collaboration with stakeholders.
• Reduce project risk through continuous feedback.
• Deliver business value early and continuously.

Key Interview Points

• Agile follows iterative and incremental development.
• Software is delivered in small working increments.
• Customer feedback is collected throughout development.
• Requirements can change even during development.
• Agile focuses on collaboration, flexibility, and continuous improvement.

Why Agile Was Introduced

Problems with Waterfall:

• Requirements are fixed at the beginning.
• Customer sees the product only after completion.
• Late feedback increases rework.
• Requirement changes are expensive.
• Higher risk of delivering an unsuitable product.

Agile Solution:

• Frequent customer collaboration.
• Early and continuous software delivery.
• Easy adaptation to changing requirements.
• Faster feedback cycle.
• Reduced project risk.

ASCII Flow

Waterfall

Requirements
      ↓
Design
      ↓
Development
      ↓
Testing
      ↓
Deployment

(Customer feedback comes at the end)

--------------------------------------------------

Agile

Plan
  ↓
Develop
  ↓
Test
  ↓
Release
  ↓
Customer Feedback
  ↓
Next Iteration

(Feedback is continuous)

Real-Life Example

Suppose a banking application is being developed.

Waterfall:
The customer requests only UPI payment initially.
After six months, they ask for Net Banking.
Adding the new feature becomes costly because the project is already
completed.

Agile:
The application is developed in small iterations.
After the first release, the customer requests Net Banking.
The feature is added in the next iteration with minimal disruption.

Industry Example

An e-commerce company releases a shopping application with basic payment
features. Based on customer feedback, Wallet, UPI, EMI, and Buy Now Pay
Later are added in future iterations instead of waiting for one final
release.

